`--depends-on` is board-local. In a fleet the most common real interruption is 'this cannot be done until X is fixed somewhere else', and that X lives in another repository.

The escalation protocol an agent follows today needs only a convention:
1. `karr create` in the other repo, tagged `escalated-from:<repo>#<id>`
2. `karr edit <id> --block \"needs <repo>#<id>: <reason>\"` in its own
3. release the claim, exit

That works, but nothing verifies it and nothing unblocks the waiting card when the other one closes. Make the link explicit so both ends can be resolved.

Order is discovered, not planned — which is why work rotates across repositories instead of finishing one before starting the next. A newly discovered prerequisite moves forward in the rotation rather than breaking a plan.

Design: `karr get-refs refs/karr-foundation/spec/fleet-execution.md`
Done, commit bd3fe3e on main (local, not pushed).

