DECISION 1 -- WHAT THE CARD CARRIES: a board NAME and an id, `BOARD#ID`, and nothing else. Anything path-shaped is refused by parse_ref, not accepted-and-warned: the card is shared state, two clones of the same fleet have the same card and different directories, so a path is wrong on every machine but the one that wrote it. The name is the repository's directory basename -- the name karr-foundation --status already prints and the name the spec's escalated-from:<repo>#<id> convention already used, so no new naming authority was invented.

DECISION 2 -- WHAT THE LOCAL CONFIG CONTRIBUTES: only the directory. `--board NAME=PATH` first, then ~/.config/karr-foundation/config.yml (dirs: outright, scan: children), matched on basename; --fleet-config relocates it (not --config: BoardDiscovery already owns that accessor). Why that file and not a new one: it is what the spec means by local configuration, it already carries this machine's view of the fleet, and a second local file describing the same fleet is a second thing to keep in step -- the argument #189 used for resolving the hub exactly once. Unknown and ambiguous names are ANSWERS, not failures: exit stays 0 and the sentence says how to place the board, because a machine holding four repositories of a six-repository fleet still has an honest report about the four.

DECISION 3 -- NO NEW FRONTMATTER FIELD. The link is a tag (needs:BOARD#ID / escalated-from:BOARD#ID). kanban-md unmarshals into a Go struct and marshals back out of it, so a key it does not model is DROPPED the first time it writes the card: a frontmatter field would lose the link through exactly the interop bridge the shared format exists for, while tags is modelled on both sides. depends_on cannot carry it at all (IntSlice over there -- a string makes the card unreadable, not merely lossy). Cost stated in the POD rather than hidden: --add-tag bypasses the validation and --remove-tag can break a link; the typed doors are the documented ones, and explicitness comes from karr parsing/verifying/resolving it, not from the slot.

DECISION 4 -- depends_on STILL BLOCKS NOTHING, and neither does a cross-board link. Role::DependencyCheck and Foundation::Picker are untouched in that respect: pick hands the card over and warns (#123), Picker still does not filter (#185), so foundation does not become stricter than the board it coordinates. What holds the waiting card is the `blocked` flag the escalating agent sets on purpose -- the link is the FACT, blocked is the DECISION -- and lifting it when the card's last link settles is the half that was missing. DependencyCheck gained one warning line for cross-board links (path-free on purpose: it says what the card waits on and points at `karr needs`, it does not reach into another repository during a status change).

DECISION 5 -- WHAT SETTLES A LINK: one of the FAR board's own terminal statuses (#67), never a hardcoded done. A far card that does NOT EXIST settles nothing -- kanban-md treats a missing local dependency as satisfied, karr already declines that for depends_on (#123) and declines it harder here: unblocking because the ticket somebody waited for cannot be found is the silent wrong answer. --resolve prints the block reason it lifted, and the write goes through update_task_guarded, so it is CAS'd and lands in the activity log like every other mutation.

VERIFICATION (the "nothing verifies it" half): link_state takes the near card as origin and reports verified only when the far card carries the matching escalated-from back-reference; a back-reference naming a DIFFERENT card is printed rather than swallowed. Nothing is fetched -- the far board is read as it stands in that working copy, because pulling somebody else's repository from inside a command here would be transport nobody asked for, and a fleet run syncs each repo as it reaches it.

WHY NOT THE HUB (#191's hint considered): the two ends of a cross-board link live in two different repositories, so the asker and the resolver never write the same ref BY CONSTRUCTION -- the conflict rule that forced #191's answer into its own ref cannot bite here. Putting the link in refs/karr-foundation/* would have added a hub dependency to both ends without removing the path problem (the hub is a path too), so the card carries it and only the directory is local.

